\section{Finding field names and values}{


Only the fields a layer publishes as \emph{queryables} can be filtered on.
\code{\link[=pdok_list_queryables]{pdok_list_queryables()}} lists them for one layer;
\code{pdok_list_layers(dataset, queryables = TRUE)} shows them for every layer
of a dataset at once. The \emph{values} a field holds are not part of the API:
consult the dataset's own documentation (for the BAG, the
\href{https://imbag.github.io/catalogus/}{Catalogus BAG} defines the permitted
values of fields such as \code{gebruiksdoel} and \code{status}), or read a small
sample of the layer with \code{\link[=pdok_read]{pdok_read()}} and inspect the column.
}

\section{Long \code{\%in\%} vectors}{


PDOK's servers reject over-long request URLs, so a \code{\%in\%} over more than
300 values (after deduplication) is sent as multiple requests of 300 values
each, and \code{\link[=pdok_read]{pdok_read()}} combines the results. This happens automatically;
it does require that the \code{\%in\%} is combined with any other conditions by
\code{&} (not inside \code{|} or \code{!}), and at most one \code{\%in\%} per filter can be this
long. Row order follows the service, not your input vector.
}

